  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "- A monitor, as `IMonitor.New` and `IAwaitableMonitor.New` create it, is four allocations and 144 bytes instead of nine and 384, so code can create one for each of a great many objects. The objects that release its lock call the monitor directly instead of through a delegate each, it locks on the object wrapping the .NET `Monitor` instead of on a separate object, it needs no lock to record lock timeouts, and its list of them starts out as the shared empty one. Taking and releasing a lock still allocates nothing.\n- Fixed: when taking a monitor's lock timed out, the exception's message named the monitor's default timeout, even when the call had passed a timeout of its own.",
